WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:06.860
So for setting up the controls for our car, we're going to be using the enhanced input system.

2
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:13.140
Now, the enhanced input system uses two primary assets.

3
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:17.640
There is the input action and the input mapping context.

4
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.140
And we use these to drive our throttle, our steering and our brakes.

5
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:26.140
Now, as it says here, the input action is a representation of something that your player can do.

6
00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:31.269
So for us, it's like our throttle, steering and brakes, so accelerating and steering and braking.

7
00:00:31.269 --> 00:00:36.770
And the input mapping context is sort of the parent of these input actions.

8
00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:42.770
And you use that to describe the rules for what can trigger any of the input actions.

9
00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:45.770
So let's take a look and see how we get those things set up.

10
00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:54.179
So back in our engine, I'm just going to come back one folder and create a new folder just to try and keep things organized.

11
00:00:54.179 --> 00:01:00.320
And I'll just call this controls.

12
00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.319
And inside here, we're going to create two input actions.

13
00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:11.299
One will be for our throttle and our steering and the other will be for our handbrake.

14
00:01:11.299 --> 00:01:18.219
So if I right click and I come to input, we have input action as an option here.

15
00:01:18.219 --> 00:01:26.780
So we'll create one of those. I'll call that IA throttle steering.

16
00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:33.980
And if I control D to duplicate that, I'll call that IA brake.

17
00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:40.980
So this will represent the ability to accelerate and to steer.

18
00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.980
And this will represent the ability to use our handbrake basically.

19
00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:52.250
So if we open up the throttle and steering input action first,

20
00:01:52.250 --> 00:02:00.310
this is where we're going to configure the settings to determine what sort of output information this asset is going to give us.

21
00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:09.810
So for the throttle and steering, the type of output information that we want is directional information from our keyboard inputs.

22
00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:14.310
So for example, when I'm holding W, I want to be able to accelerate.

23
00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:19.810
And when I'm holding A, I want to be able to turn left, holding D, turn right.

24
00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:23.810
So this will be two different axes of information basically.

25
00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:29.469
X will be our forwards and backwards axis and Y will be our steering, our left and right axis.

26
00:02:29.469 --> 00:02:36.469
So to set that up under the value type heading here, we're going to change that to axis 2D.

27
00:02:36.469 --> 00:02:42.479
So that's a vector 2D which would give us those two axes, the X and the Y axis.

28
00:02:42.479 --> 00:02:47.479
And we want those to trigger only when we're holding these keys.

29
00:02:47.479 --> 00:02:50.479
So we don't want to just tap the key and have it infinitely accelerate.

30
00:02:50.479 --> 00:02:56.599
We want to be holding the key all the time and it only be accelerating when we're holding, the same with steering.

31
00:02:56.599 --> 00:02:59.169
So to do that, we need to add a trigger.

32
00:02:59.169 --> 00:03:05.800
So under the triggers heading here, we'll add an element and we'll change that to hold.

33
00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:09.960
And if I pop open the index, you see we get a hold time threshold here.

34
00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:16.960
And this is saying that, okay, you can hold it for one second and nothing will happen until that second has elapsed.

35
00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:20.960
So because we want it to be instant, I'm just going to change that to zero.

36
00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:26.960
Otherwise there'll be like a one second delay before the car will start turning left or right or start accelerating.

37
00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.449
So if I just save that now, we can move on to our braking.

38
00:03:32.449 --> 00:03:35.949
Now this is going to work in a slightly different way.

39
00:03:35.949 --> 00:03:38.449
This is going to be our handbrake.

40
00:03:38.449 --> 00:03:41.449
So we don't need any directional information from this.

41
00:03:41.449 --> 00:03:44.449
We just simply need to know, is this on or is this off?

42
00:03:44.449 --> 00:03:46.449
So is it true or is it false?

43
00:03:46.449 --> 00:03:51.449
And the default value type of a bool works perfectly for that because that's the information that it gives us.

44
00:03:51.449 --> 00:03:53.449
So we'll leave that as it is.

45
00:03:53.449 --> 00:03:56.449
And the only thing we want to do is add a trigger again.

46
00:03:56.449 --> 00:03:59.449
And we need to add the same trigger, so we just want to hold this.

47
00:03:59.449 --> 00:04:07.120
Meaning that every time we hold this, for example spacebar, the handbrake will activate.

48
00:04:07.439 --> 00:04:16.589
And again, I'm going to set the hold time threshold to zero so that, you know, there's no delay in activating the handbrake.

49
00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:21.589
So we have our two input actions set up and they're giving us the information that we need.

50
00:04:21.589 --> 00:04:25.589
So we've got the directional information for the throttle and the steering.

51
00:04:25.589 --> 00:04:35.439
And then we have the true or false information from the handbrake or the brake input action.

52
00:04:35.439 --> 00:04:37.939
And this is where we'll set up our key mapping.

53
00:04:37.939 --> 00:04:45.139
So determining which buttons on our keyboard will give us what information from the input actions.

54
00:04:45.139 --> 00:04:49.180
So let's create our input mapping context.

55
00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:53.180
So right clicking back up to input and selecting input mapping context.

56
00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:56.180
I'm going to call this IMC.

57
00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:58.180
I'll just put controls.

58
00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:05.449
And if I open that up, you see there's not much in here until we start adding mappings.

59
00:05:05.449 --> 00:05:11.449
So if we add one mapping, this will essentially look for the input actions that you have in your project.

60
00:05:11.449 --> 00:05:20.490
So we've got the brake, if I close that down, and we've got the throttle and steering.

61
00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:22.490
Now the brake is quite a simple setup.

62
00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:28.709
So if I just pop that open, press the keyboard icon here and then press spacebar.

63
00:05:28.709 --> 00:05:30.709
That'll input the key mapping that we want.

64
00:05:30.709 --> 00:05:37.420
You could also search for it in here if it's not on the keyboard, it's on the gamepad or something, for example.

65
00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:40.420
But we don't need to add any triggers because we've already set that up.

66
00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:47.420
And we don't need to add any modifiers because the information that we want from this is just the true or false bool.

67
00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:55.420
So when we're pressing this button, so spacebar, we want it to give us the true output, which it will by default.

68
00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:58.420
And when we let go, it will give us the false output.

69
00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:00.420
So there's nothing more we need to do for that.

70
00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:09.069
In the throttle and steering, however, we're going to need more than just one input key.

71
00:06:09.069 --> 00:06:11.069
We're going to need W, A, S and D.

72
00:06:11.069 --> 00:06:17.389
We're going to need four of them so we can drive forward and backwards and then steer left and right.

73
00:06:17.389 --> 00:06:29.339
So if I add two more of those, just minimize them a little bit, we have W, S, A and D.

74
00:06:29.339 --> 00:06:36.879
So these are the keys that we want to use to extract different information from the input action.

75
00:06:36.879 --> 00:06:39.879
And if you remember, it was the vector 2D.

76
00:06:39.879 --> 00:06:43.879
So that's the X axis information and the Y axis information.

77
00:06:43.879 --> 00:06:49.389
So our X axis is going to be our W and S, our forwards and backwards.

78
00:06:49.389 --> 00:06:54.389
And our Y axis is going to be the A and the D, so our turning or steering left and right.

79
00:06:54.389 --> 00:07:00.899
By default, the information that comes out of the input action is the positive X value.

80
00:07:00.899 --> 00:07:07.899
And we know that our vehicle is facing along the positive X axis, so that's going to be the forward direction for our vehicle.

81
00:07:07.899 --> 00:07:16.060
So that means if I pop this open here, we don't need to add any triggers or modifiers to the information that's being output when we press W.

82
00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:19.060
And because we want to find that positive X value.

83
00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:21.060
And that's what we're getting by default.

84
00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:32.149
But with the S input, we do need to add a modifier because we need to find the negative X axis, which means we'll be able to reverse.

85
00:07:32.149 --> 00:07:40.439
So if we add plus here on the modifiers tab, and then choose the drop down and choose negate.

86
00:07:40.439 --> 00:07:46.439
What this is going to do is essentially invert the values that have been spat out by the input action.

87
00:07:47.439 --> 00:07:54.860
So by default, we get the positive X, if we negate that, that will give us the negative X axis.

88
00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:57.860
And that's what we're going to use to be able to reverse the vehicle.

89
00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:06.660
Next, looking at our steering with A and D, we're going to need to use a modifier to help us find the Y axis.

90
00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:14.040
So if we add a new modifier, and from the drop down box, we choose swizzle input axis values.

91
00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:22.040
Now what this is going to do is essentially convert the X axis into the Y axis and give us that Y axis output.

92
00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:31.779
But because our A key we want to turn left, we know that that information also needs to give us the negative values.

93
00:08:31.779 --> 00:08:34.779
So we can add a second modifier here.

94
00:08:34.779 --> 00:08:36.779
And from the drop down, choose negate.

95
00:08:36.779 --> 00:08:41.779
So this is going to do is find the Y axis and then invert the values.

96
00:08:41.779 --> 00:08:43.779
So give us the negative values.

97
00:08:43.779 --> 00:08:54.940
And for D, because we need the positive values, the only modifier we need to add is the swizzle input axis values modifier.

98
00:08:54.940 --> 00:09:00.070
So just to go over these again, if I pop them all open, our brake will be using spacebar,

99
00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:02.070
and we don't have any modifiers or any triggers on there.

100
00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:05.070
So it's just going to give us the true or false response.

101
00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:13.250
Then for W, we'll be getting the positive X axis, which is the default value.

102
00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:17.250
For S, we've added a modifier to flip that default value so that it's negative.

103
00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:20.250
So it gives us the negative X value.

104
00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:25.980
And then for A and D, we've added the swizzle input axis values to find the Y axis instead.

105
00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:31.980
And just for A, we've also added in a gate modifier so that we can flip those Y axis.

106
00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:36.980
So they have one that turns in the positive direction and one that turns in the negative direction.

107
00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:41.460
Now that we have the logic set up for our input actions,

108
00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:46.460
we can go back to our vehicle blueprint and create the logic to read this information

109
00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:50.460
and use it to drive things like the throttle and the brakes and the steering

110
00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:52.460
and actually get our vehicle moving.

111
00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:59.539
Back inside our vehicle blueprint here, if we open up the event graph,

112
00:09:59.539 --> 00:10:03.919
this is where we're going to be setting up all of the logic to trigger certain events

113
00:10:03.919 --> 00:10:06.919
like our throttle and steering and braking

114
00:10:06.919 --> 00:10:10.919
with the key presses that we just set up in the input mapping context.

115
00:10:10.919 --> 00:10:16.049
Now the first thing we need to do is enable the input mapping context that we just created

116
00:10:16.049 --> 00:10:20.049
so that the blueprint knows where to read the information from.

117
00:10:20.049 --> 00:10:23.340
And we're going to do that on event begin play.

118
00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:26.340
So we don't need these two events here, we're just going to delete those.

119
00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:31.340
And I'll drag off event begin play and I'll cast to, or search for,

120
00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:34.340
cast to player controller.

121
00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:43.419
And this is going to access the player controller that we input here.

122
00:10:43.419 --> 00:10:47.679
And the target for this is going to be itself, so this blueprint.

123
00:10:47.679 --> 00:10:50.679
So if I just search for get controller,

124
00:10:50.679 --> 00:10:56.379
I default the target itself unless we put something else into this input pin here.

125
00:10:56.379 --> 00:11:02.570
And next we need to actually find the enhanced input local player subsystem,

126
00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:05.570
which is the controller system we're going to reference.

127
00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:15.070
So if I right click search for enhanced input local player subsystem,

128
00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:20.220
it's actually the other one that we needed there.

129
00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:27.220
So enhanced input local player subsystem, there we go.

130
00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:31.250
And then we need to make sure that this is valid.

131
00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:34.250
So if we drag up and search for is valid,

132
00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:39.409
we need to use the is valid with the question mark just here, the query.

133
00:11:39.409 --> 00:11:41.950
And then I'll just connect that logic up.

134
00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:49.320
The last thing we need to do is add the mapping context off the end of this.

135
00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:55.320
So if this is valid, we want it to reference the input mapping context that we just created.

136
00:11:55.320 --> 00:12:04.539
So if I right click search for add mapping context,

137
00:12:04.539 --> 00:12:08.549
under the dropdown heading here,

138
00:12:08.549 --> 00:12:12.549
it's going to give us access to all of the input mapping context assets within the project.

139
00:12:12.549 --> 00:12:14.549
And obviously it's just the one that we created.

140
00:12:14.549 --> 00:12:17.549
So just at the top of the list here, we'll set that up.

141
00:12:17.549 --> 00:12:25.029
And then we'll actually add the target and connect that to the enhanced input local player subsystem.

142
00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:30.340
If I compile and save that.

143
00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:34.750
With this logic, the blueprint is now reading from the mapping context that we've created.

144
00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:38.750
So any logic we do from now on will be based on these inputs.

145
00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:44.259
So let's set up the logic for our braking system first.

146
00:12:44.259 --> 00:12:49.740
So I'm just going to right click and search for our action input.

147
00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:53.019
Oops.

148
00:12:53.019 --> 00:13:02.570
And we want to search for the AI brake, sorry, IA brake enhanced input action.

149
00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:08.590
And when this is triggered, we want to set the handbrake input.

150
00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:13.490
So if I search for set handbrake input,

151
00:13:16.139 --> 00:13:20.139
the target is going to be the vehicle movement component.

152
00:13:20.139 --> 00:13:24.139
So what we can do actually is just drag that into the world here,

153
00:13:24.139 --> 00:13:29.360
into the graph rather and have that be our target.

154
00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:35.360
And the input is going to be the action value that is given out when we press and release the space bar.

155
00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:39.360
So the key that we inputted as our input action.

156
00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:44.220
And if we just connect those up together there.

157
00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:47.220
That's essentially all the logic we need to do for the braking system.

158
00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:49.220
It's quite straightforward.

159
00:13:49.220 --> 00:13:52.220
When it comes to the steering and the throttle, it gets a little bit more complex

160
00:13:52.220 --> 00:13:57.570
because we want to essentially break out the individual axes, X and Y,

161
00:13:57.570 --> 00:14:01.570
as well as the positive and negative variants of those.

162
00:14:01.570 --> 00:14:07.240
So the first thing we'll do is find the throttle and steering input.

163
00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:13.409
And you can see we have the same options here.

164
00:14:13.409 --> 00:14:18.409
We have our action value and the triggered pin we're going to use these two.

165
00:14:18.409 --> 00:14:23.269
So our action value is, as you can see, is a vector 2D,

166
00:14:23.269 --> 00:14:29.269
which means it's giving off more information than our bool above for the braking system.

167
00:14:29.269 --> 00:14:33.269
And that means we're going to need to sort of break that information into its two channels,

168
00:14:33.269 --> 00:14:35.269
so into the X and the Y channels.

169
00:14:35.269 --> 00:14:40.269
So I'm going to pull off there and search for brake vector 2D.

170
00:14:40.269 --> 00:14:43.850
And that will give us the X and the Y channels.

171
00:14:43.850 --> 00:14:51.100
So now we have access to what we set up for the W and the S keyboard inputs

172
00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:57.100
and the A and the D keyboard inputs, which will affect the Y channel or the Y axis.

173
00:14:57.100 --> 00:15:02.490
So now that we've split this information, what we can do is start setting up the throttle.

174
00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:08.129
So I'm going to search for set throttle input.

175
00:15:09.129 --> 00:15:16.110
And again, dragging the vehicle movement component as our target.

176
00:15:16.110 --> 00:15:22.470
And we want to connect the X axis here,

177
00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:30.340
because the default X axis is what we're going to use as our W, our forward, our throttle input.

178
00:15:30.340 --> 00:15:32.340
So let's just connect up that logic.

179
00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:42.580
Now we set up the reverse with the brake input event or function, should I say.

180
00:15:42.580 --> 00:15:51.549
So if I search for set brake input rather than the set handbrake input,

181
00:15:51.549 --> 00:16:03.870
as long as on your vehicle component we have reverse as brake checked,

182
00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:09.899
using this set brake input will actually both slow the car down if it's going forwards

183
00:16:09.899 --> 00:16:14.899
and then enter reverse if you continue to hold S in this case.

184
00:16:14.899 --> 00:16:17.899
But that's only valid when we use the negative values.

185
00:16:17.899 --> 00:16:27.639
So to access the negative values on the X axis, we will multiply the output by minus one

186
00:16:27.639 --> 00:16:29.639
and then connect that logic up there.

187
00:16:29.639 --> 00:16:32.730
And we'll make sure we connect the execute as well.

188
00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:37.649
And the next thing we can set up is the steering.

189
00:16:37.649 --> 00:16:40.870
And that reads directly from the Y output.

190
00:16:40.870 --> 00:16:45.100
So what I'm going to do here, just shift those over a little bit

191
00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:49.100
and search for set steering input.

192
00:16:49.100 --> 00:17:00.379
I connect those guys up and drag the Y pin over to our steering input here.

193
00:17:00.379 --> 00:17:05.730
Just going to double click that so we get a couple of reroute nodes

194
00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:11.380
that we can use to just see the information a little more clearly.

195
00:17:11.380 --> 00:17:14.380
And that's it as far as it goes for our input actions.

196
00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:21.420
So we're setting our throttle input with the positive X value.

197
00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:24.420
Then we're setting our brake, which we're also using as reverse

198
00:17:24.420 --> 00:17:30.220
because we've got the use brake as reverse option ticked.

199
00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:32.220
I just search for that just to make sure.

200
00:17:32.220 --> 00:17:34.980
Reverse as brake.

201
00:17:34.980 --> 00:17:38.269
And we're using the negative X axis for that.

202
00:17:38.269 --> 00:17:43.940
And then we're setting our steering input based on the Y values.

203
00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:45.940
So we've set up our input actions.

204
00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:47.940
We've determined which buttons on our keyboard

205
00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:51.940
we want to trigger these different actions with our mapping context.

206
00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:54.940
And our blueprint is now reading from all of our information

207
00:17:54.940 --> 00:17:59.420
to set our brakes and our throttle and our steering.

208
00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:03.420
The final thing we need to do is to tell the project to use this information

209
00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:06.670
when we press play in the level.

210
00:18:06.670 --> 00:18:09.960
And we do that with a game mode blueprint.

211
00:18:09.960 --> 00:18:11.960
So coming back into our content browser here,

212
00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:14.960
if I right click and we create a new blueprint,

213
00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:19.539
we'll choose game mode base as the parent class.

214
00:18:19.539 --> 00:18:25.819
And I'm just going to call this gm underscore, let's just say driving.

215
00:18:25.819 --> 00:18:29.230
And double click this to open.

216
00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:31.230
Inside here in the details panel,

217
00:18:31.230 --> 00:18:35.230
all we need to change is the default pawn class.

218
